The scenario's JSON is gated against the drake it came from

The forest corpus landed at 87a2bb9 with three artifacts per type and four gates over them, and a
hole between the first two artifacts that none of the four could see.

WHAT WAS UNGATED

`ScenarioGenTest` walks `.json` only — it decodes each one and asserts that `Generator.generate`
reproduces the `.scala` beside it. `ScenarioDrakeTest` walks `.drake` only and asks whether
`emit(parse(x)) == x`, which is a question about the surface being self-consistent, not about the
surface agreeing with anything. Nothing read a `.drake` and its `.json` together. Hand-edit a
scenario `.json` away from its surface and all 531 tests stay green: the projection gate happily
projects the edited definition, the drake gate happily round-trips the untouched surface, and the
two never meet.

`src/main` does not have this hole. `DrakeGenTest` pins `emit(X.json) == X.drake` per file, which
ties the pair in the emit direction — correct there, because that corpus was JSON-first and the
surface was derived from it. The scenario is the other way round: the commit message for 87a2bb9
says the JSON was made by parsing each drake. So the direction that matches how the artifacts were
actually produced is the parse direction, and that is what this gate asserts —

    parse(X.drake) == X.json     for each of the 23 files

— placed ahead of the projection gate rather than after it, so the suite now reads in the order the
trio is made: drake, JSON, Scala, the thing running. The header's four gates became five and the
numbering moved with them.

VALUES, NOT TEXT

The committed files are canonical-printer output (`Printer.spaces2` with `colonLeft = ""`, the same
printer `DrakeCLI parse` prints with), so a text comparison would pass today and would have given a
line diff for free. It is still the wrong gate. The printer is a formatting choice, and pinning it
would make one changed separator read as twenty-three regressions — a gate that fails for reasons
it does not care about teaches sessions to skim it. Circe's object equality is order-insensitive,
so comparing `Json` values pins what is meant: the normative artifact says what the surface says.
Both sides are logged through the canonical printer on failure, so the expectation can be pasted
straight into the file it is about.

The comparison is against the RAW file JSON rather than the decoded-and-re-encoded definition,
which incidentally closes a second silent hole: `definitions` builds its list with `.toOption`, so
a `.json` that no longer decodes to a `TypeDefinition` simply vanishes from the projection gate
rather than failing it. Every scenario `.json` has a `.drake`, so every one of them now goes
through a comparison that a decode failure cannot survive.

